      "topic": "Yield Curve Inversion and Recession Signals",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Did you know an inverted yield curve has preceded nearly every US recession, yet it predicts timing poorly? When short-term Treasury yields exceed long-term yields, the curve flips downward, signaling tighter policy and slower growth expectations. Since 1969, every US recession has followed an inversion, according to Federal Reserve history, but false signals can linger for months. In India watch the RBI repo rate, government bond yields, Nifty b",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
    "D01-15:00": {
      "row_index": 3,
      "day": 1,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-20T15:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Free Cash Flow vs Net Profit",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Most investors don't realize that a company can report massive profits while its bank account slowly bleeds dry every single day. Net profit includes non-cash accounting items like depreciation. Free cash flow shows actual money left after paying all necessary capital expenditures. Look at many high growth tech firms. They show brilliant accounting earnings yet burn billions in negative free cash flow annually. On the NSE, Indian manufacturing gi",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
    "D01-20:00": {
      "row_index": 4,
      "day": 1,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-20T20:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) Basics",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Most investors don't realize that a company is actually worth just the present value of its future cash flows. Money tomorrow is worth less than money today. We use a discount rate to shrink future earnings down into today's actual value. If you apply a higher discount rate, perceived risk shoots up, drastically reducing the total calculated valuation of the entire business. When RBI interest rates shift across India, the discount rate changes, d",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
    "D02-09:00": {
      "row_index": 5,
      "day": 2,
      "scheduled_at": "2026-10-21T09:00:00+05:30",
      "topic": "P/E Ratio and Earnings Yield",
      "status": "Done",
      "attempts": 0,
      "job_id": null,
      "claimed_at": null,
      "youtube_video_id": null,
      "youtube_url": null,
      "published_at": null,
      "last_error": null,
      "script_full": "Most investors don't realize that the classic price-to-earnings ratio is actually just the inverse of your earnings yield. If a company trades at a P/E of twenty, its earnings yield is five percent, calculated by dividing one by twenty. You must compare this stock earnings yield directly against risk-free bond yields to see if equities are genuinely attractive. In the Indian markets, when NSE stock P/E ratios soar high, earnings yields drop below",
      "note": "imported from Excel"
    },
